<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
  <!--
    MABS/OutSystems builds never apply the Crashlytics Gradle plugin
    (com.google.firebase:firebase-crashlytics-gradle), because MABS owns the Gradle
    toolchain, and that plugin is the only thing that generates a Crashlytics build ID
    (the R8 mapping file id, also written to assets/crashlytics-build.properties).

    Without it, firebase-crashlytics 18/19 aborts the whole Firebase initialization:
    FirebaseInitProvider -> FirebaseApp.initializeApp -> CrashlyticsCore.onPreExecute
    throws "java.lang.IllegalStateException: The Crashlytics build ID is missing. This
    occurs when the Crashlytics Gradle plugin is missing from your app's build
    configuration." and the app crashes on every launch, before any Cordova code runs.

    CrashlyticsCore.onPreExecute() reads this exact boolean resource (verified with javap
    against firebase-crashlytics 19.0.3: CommonUtils.getBooleanResourceValue(context,
    "com.crashlytics.RequireBuildId", true), and isBuildIdValid() returns true
    immediately when it is false, logging "Configured not to require a build ID.").
    Setting it to false lets Crashlytics initialize without a build ID, so Firebase
    Analytics, Cloud Messaging, Remote Config, Performance and the plugin's own
    recordException()/log()/setUserId() calls all keep working. Crash reports are still
    recorded and uploaded, but they cannot be symbolicated against a mapping file
    because no mapping file id exists.

    If you ever manage to apply the Crashlytics Gradle plugin in the MABS build (see the
    commented block in src/android/build.gradle), remove this file/entry: the plugin then
    supplies a real build ID, and leaving this override in place risks a duplicate
    resource error.
  -->
  <bool name="com.crashlytics.RequireBuildId">false</bool>
</resources>